@@s001 Infinite lists and unfold
@@s002 dato:state,ora:f at work,ok:value emitted,no:None = end
@@s003 <code>unfold(0)(f)</code>: one starts from the <b>state</b> 0. At each step f looks at the state and says what to emit and what the state will be afterwards.
@@s004 f(0) = Some((0, 1)): it emits <b>0</b>, new state 1.
@@s005 f(1) = Some((10, 2)): it emits <b>10</b>.
@@s006 f(2) = Some((20, 3)): it emits <b>20</b>.
@@s007 f(3) = <b>None</b>: the list ends. <code>.toList</code> = List(0, 10, 20). The state serves to remember, the value is what comes out.
@@s008 ok:cell built,due:tail not yet forced
@@s009 ok:emitted,dato:a,ora:b
@@s010 ora:print,no:extra computation
@@s011 dato:state (list, k),ok:emitted,no:end
@@s012 Infinite lists and unfold
@@s013 Exercises 1, 10–13. An infinite list is defined by a function that <b>calls itself in the tail</b>: thanks to <code>cons</code> the call does not start until it is needed. <code>unfold</code> generalises the idea: from an initial state and a rule "next value and next state" it generates the whole list.
@@s014 <code>ones</code>, <code>from</code>, <code>to</code>, <code>naturals</code>
@@s015 Exercise 1 and the example of the slide
@@s016 An infinite list of 1s that points to <b>itself</b>. It works because the second argument of <code>cons</code> is by-name: <code>ones</code> is not read now.
@@s017 <code>from(n)</code>: the head is <code>n</code>, the tail is "from n + 1 on". The recursive call is inside <code>cons</code>, so it does not start.
@@s018 <code>to(n)</code>: the same, going down. This too is infinite (towards the negatives).
@@s019 The naturals are <code>from(1)</code>: the course test <code>Ex01.06</code> wants the head to be <b>1</b> (with <code>from(0)</code> it fails: <code>Falsified after 0 passed tests</code>). <code>lazy val</code> in the companion object: computed at first use.
@@s020 A recursive function without a base case?
@@s021 On ordinary lists it would be an infinite loop. Here not: each call builds <b>one</b> cell and stops. The next cell is born only when someone forces the tail. <code>naturals.toList</code> instead never terminates: it wants everything.
@@s022 Infinite lists: one cell at a time
@@s023 <code>fibs</code>: infinite Fibonacci (exercise 10)
@@s024 Same idea as week 1: for the next number the last two are enough. Here however I do not return a number: I produce the list of all of them.
@@s025 fibs.take(6).toList
@@s026 I emit <code>a</code>, then the window slides.
@@s027 b becomes a, a + b becomes b.
@@s028 Checked. The type, which the file leaves as <code>Any</code>, is <code>LazyList[Int]</code>.
@@s029 go(a, b): the window that emits
@@s030 <code>unfold</code>: the general generator (exercise 11)
@@s031 Reading the signature
@@s032 <code>S</code> is the type of the <b>state</b>: what I must remember between one step and the next. <code>z</code> is the initial state.
@@s033 <code>f</code> looks at the state and answers with a <b>pair</b>: the next value to emit (<code>A</code>) and the new state (<code>S</code>).
@@s034 
@@s035 It is inside an Option: <code>None</code> means "the list ends here".
@@s036 The result is the list of the emitted values, one per step.
@@s037 Without pattern matching: map and getOrElse of week 3
@@s038 I ask <code>f</code> what to do with the current state: I get an <code>Option[(A, S)]</code>.
@@s039 If there is a pair, I open it: <code>a</code> the value, <code>s</code> the new state. (Scala 3 allows writing <code>(a, s) =></code> for a function that receives a pair.)
@@s040 I emit <code>a</code> and, as a lazy tail, the <code>unfold</code> from the new state.
@@s041 If <code>f</code> said <code>None</code>, the list is empty. The result of <code>map</code> is an <code>Option[LazyList[A]]</code>: <code>getOrElse</code> "opens" it.
@@s042 unfold(0)(n => if n < 3 then Some((n * 10, n + 1)) else None).toList
@@s043 Emits 0, new state 1.
@@s044 Emits 10.
@@s045 Emits 20.
@@s046 End.
@@s047 The state serves to remember; the value is what comes out.
@@s048 State after state
@@s049 Why <code>unfold</code>?
@@s050 It does the same thing as the recursion written by hand, but the recursion is written by it: you describe <b>one step only</b>.
@@s051 By hand
@@s052 With unfold
@@s053 Two things change: where you wrote <code>cons(valore, &lt;ricorsione&gt;)</code> you write <code>Some((valore, nuovoStato))</code>; where you wrote <code>empty</code> you write <code>None</code>. <code>cons</code> and the recursive call are put by <code>unfold</code>, in its last line: <code>case Some((a, s)) => cons(a, unfold(s)(f))</code>.
@@s054 The twin of fold
@@s055 <code>foldRight</code> <b>consumes</b> a list and gives a value (exercise 8). <code>unfold</code> starts from a value (the state) and <b>builds</b> a list (exercise 13). In both cases you do not write the recursion: in the course's style scale it is the step from <i>forgivable</i> to <i>enlightened</i>.
@@s056 Doubt: is unfold official like cons?
@@s057 No. In the file the teacher wrote <code>empty</code>, <code>cons</code>, <code>apply</code> (companion) and <code>headOption</code>, <code>tail</code>, <code>foldRight</code>, <code>foldLeft</code>, <code>find</code> (in the enum). <code>unfold</code> you wrote it in exercise 11. The standard library's <code>LazyList</code>, which the course does <b>not</b> use, has one of its own: <code>scala.collection.immutable.LazyList.unfold(1)(n => if n &gt; 4 then None else Some((n, n * 2)))</code> → <code>List(1, 2, 4)</code> (checked).
@@s058 Measured: unfold is one step ahead
@@s059 ora:computed before necessary
@@s060 I print inside the user's f and ask for 2 elements out of 3: <code>.map(…)</code> against <code>.mapUnfold(…)</code>, then <code>.take(2).toList</code>.
@@s061 Same list, same function.
@@s062 <code>unfold</code> must call <code>f</code> <b>first</b>, to know whether the answer is <code>None</code> (finite list) or <code>Some</code>. <code>foldRight</code> + <code>cons</code> instead keeps the head suspended too. Real output.
@@s063 map against mapUnfold: who computes first
