@@s083 The versions with unfold (checked)
@@s084 The state is the window (a, b). I emit <code>a</code>, the new state is <code>(b, a + b)</code>. <code>fibsUnfold.take(100).toList == fibs.take(100).toList</code> is true.
@@s085 The state is "the part of the list I still have to read". The braces with some <code>case</code>s are a function that does pattern matching on its argument.
@@s086 The state is a pair: the list and how many elements are missing. Write the double brackets <code>unfold((this, n))</code>: it is seen to be <b>one</b> argument, the state.
@@s087 Where does <code>k</code> come from? You do not declare it: it is born in the pattern, when the <code>case</code> takes the pair apart. <code>n</code> stays fixed (it is the parameter of the method); what goes down is the counter <b>inside the state</b>.
@@s088 <code>case _</code> collects the two ways of ending: empty list <b>or</b> counter at 0. It goes <b>last</b>: the cases are tried from the top.
@@s089 <code>takeWhileUnfold</code>: like <code>mapUnfold</code>, but the guard decides whether to continue. Predicate false → one falls into <code>case _</code> → end.
@@s090 The text chooses a non-strict operation: <code>ope</code> can ignore an argument without computing it (useful with <code>||</code> and <code>&amp;&amp;</code>).
@@s091 The state is the two lists. It stops as soon as one ends.
@@s092 Test case of the text
@@s093 Result (checked)
@@s094 <code>naturals.zipWith[Int, Int](_+_)(naturals).take(2000000000).take(5).toList</code>
@@s095 <code>List(2, 4, 6, 8, 10)</code>
@@s096 <code>naturals.map(_%2==0).zipWith[Boolean, Boolean](_||_)(naturals.map(_%2==1)).take(4).toList</code>
@@s097 <code>List(true, true, true, true)</code>
@@s098 The second: every number is even <b>or</b> odd, so always <code>true</code>. Think about it before running, as the text asks.
@@s099 Exercise 13: what the two test cases must give
@@s100 "naturals.zipWith(_ + _)(naturals) adds each number to itself, so it gives the even numbers 2, 4, 6, …; take(2000000000) costs nothing, because it builds one node at a time. The Boolean one gives only true: every number is either even or odd. Since ope takes its operands by name, || never evaluates the second one once the first is true."
